window.NOTES = {
 "1": {
  "title": "DiscoLoop (title)",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: title, abstract</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> Hi everyone, I'm Hengyu. Today I'll share our new paper, DiscoLoop: looping discrete embeddings and continuous hidden states for multi-hop reasoning. This is joint work with Tianyu Guo, Zixuan Wang and Hanlin Zhu, all equal contribution, advised by Jason Lee, Jiantao Jiao, Stuart Russell and Song Mei.</li><li><em>(optional; it got a laugh last time)</em> Funny timing: right after we posted the paper this summer, a new startup was announced with the same name, DiscoLoop. That made our PR a bit hard.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> Before the task itself: why should we care about reasoning without a chain of thought?</p>"
 },
 "2": {
  "title": "GPT-6 Astra thinks without writing it down",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: none. Source: OpenAI, GPT-6 Astra System Card (2026), Table 10</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> I think we have all been shocked by GPT-6 Astra's reasoning, like solving the Navier–Stokes equations. Sam said it \"feels like the Manhattan Project\". One feature that sets Astra apart from earlier models: it can reason without writing the reasoning down. Here is an example from Astra's own system card.</li><li data-steps=\"1-1\"><strong>step 1</strong> <em>(question card)</em>: The question is: which official was known as the \"masked man on the white horse\"? And the instruction says: do not reason about this question in your analysis; think about anything else.</li><li data-steps=\"2-2\"><strong>step 2</strong> <em>(three chains of thought)</em>: Here are the chains of thought of three models on the same prompt.</li><li data-steps=\"3-3\"><strong>step 3</strong> <em>(highlights)</em>: GPT-5.5 Thinking and GPT-5.6 Sol can't help it. Their CoT still reasons about the question, and even names the answer: William Clark, Reagan's Interior Secretary.</li><li data-steps=\"4-4\"><strong>step 4</strong> <em>(Astra column)</em>: GPT-6 Astra follows the instruction. Its CoT is about sunlight on a wooden desk and a ceramic mug, nothing related to the question. And it still answers correctly.</li><li data-steps=\"5-5\"><strong>step 5</strong> <em>(definition)</em>: So Astra can do implicit reasoning: reasoning without writing the intermediate steps into the context. The steps happen inside the forward pass.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> But not every model can do this.</p><p><strong>Q&amp;A from the first talk</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 2, at step 5): The CoT has nothing about the question, so where is the reasoning? Is this a hidden CoT?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> This is Astra's actual CoT. OpenAI published it in the system card (users normally can't see raw CoTs; OpenAI can). It says nothing about the question, yet the answer is right, so the computation happens in the hidden states, not in readable text. <em>(Extra, if pushed: unrelated tokens still give the model extra positions, so extra compute, and models can use even meaningless filler tokens (Pfau et al. 2024, cited in our paper). Either way the reasoning is not written down, and that is what we call implicit.)</em></li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (after the talk): Your work doesn't really explain how Astra does this, right?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Right. Astra is motivation, not something we analyze. It shows that reasoning without CoT is possible; our question is which architecture can do it, and looping is a strong hint.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (after the talk): A model could also just write a long CoT.<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Yes, that is the other route. But long CoTs are inefficient: verbose, expensive on long-horizon tasks, especially in RL. Reasoning inside the forward pass, for example with loops, is a way to compress the response length.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Suggestion</strong> (Speaker 2, after the talk): reproduce an Astra-style no-CoT test to show that a looped architecture really answers such questions without CoT. (Good future experiment: real two-hop factual questions, looped vs. non-looped LM at matched compute.)</li></ul>"
 },
 "3": {
  "title": "Jev: knows both facts, cannot compose them",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: none. Source: Jev (TypeSafe AI, 2026), jev-1.13.0, 5 trials per question</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> Another recently popular model is Jev. TypeSafe AI calls it a \"System 1\" model: fast, intuitive, structured answers, no chain of thought. So what happens if we ask it a compositional question?</li><li data-steps=\"1-1\"><strong>step 1</strong> <em>(hop 1)</em>: First hop: in what year did Nelson Mandela become president of South Africa? 1994, with probability one.</li><li data-steps=\"2-2\"><strong>step 2</strong> <em>(hop 2)</em>: Second hop: which country won the 1994 FIFA men's World Cup? Brazil, also with probability one.</li><li data-steps=\"3-3\"><strong>step 3</strong> <em>(two-hop)</em>: Now compose them: which country won the World Cup in the year Mandela became president? I tried five times, and Jev never said Brazil: Italy three times, Germany twice. The probabilities are almost uniform. It's guessing.</li><li data-steps=\"4-4\"><strong>step 4</strong> <em>(conclusion)</em>: So Jev knows both facts, but it cannot compose them without writing down the intermediate answer, 1994. Implicit multi-hop reasoning is unreliable.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> Astra can do it, Jev can't. So what is missing?</p><p><strong>Backup</strong></p><ul><li>This chain is one of 11 of the 105 two-hop chains that failed in all 5 trials.</li></ul><p><strong>Q&amp;A from the first talk</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Q</strong> (at step 3): With CoT, Jev should be able to do this, right?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Probably yes: with CoT it would write down 1994 and then look up the winner. The point is doing it without saying the bridge out loud. Astra can, Jev can't. So what is missing? That is our question.</li></ul></li></ul>"
 },
 "4": {
  "title": "Can LLMs do implicit multi-hop reasoning?",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §1 Introduction; §1.1 Related work</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> So the core question is: can LLMs do implicit multi-hop reasoning? That is, compose facts they learned in training, inside the forward pass, without writing down the intermediate answer. To study this cleanly, we use a synthetic two-hop task, the most basic form of multi-hop reasoning. Synthetic, because then we control exactly which facts the model sees, and in which role.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> Here is the task.</p><p><strong>Backup</strong></p><ul><li>This is out-of-context, in-weight reasoning (§1.1): the key facts are not in the prompt; they were learned in training and live in the weights.</li><li>The symbolic two-hop task was proposed as \"the most fundamental version of implicit reasoning\" (Wang et al. 2024; Ye et al. 2025).</li></ul>"
 },
 "5": {
  "title": "Two-hop reasoning: the task and the ID / OOD splits",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §2 (Eq. 1, two-graph design); §3.1 (dataset); Fig. 1 (top)</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> Here is the task: two-hop reasoning.</li><li data-steps=\"1-1\"><strong>step 1</strong> <em>(fact 1)</em>: Fact one: Alice's son is Bob.</li><li data-steps=\"2-2\"><strong>step 2</strong> <em>(fact 2)</em>: Fact two: Bob's wife is Carol.</li><li data-steps=\"3-3\"><strong>step 3</strong> <em>(question)</em>: The two-hop question: who is Alice's son's wife?</li><li data-steps=\"4-6\"><strong>steps 4–6</strong> <em>(compose, answer, bridge)</em>: We compose the two facts through Bob and get Carol. We call Bob the bridge entity.</li><li data-steps=\"7-7\"><strong>step 7</strong> <em>(names become symbols)</em>: In training, every name is an entity token and every relation is a relation token, so the chain becomes a, r<sub>1</sub>, b, r<sub>2</sub>, c.</li><li data-steps=\"8-8\"><strong>step 8</strong> <em>(atomic fact)</em>: An atomic fact is a three-token sequence: a, r<sub>1</sub>, b. The model predicts the last token, b.</li><li data-steps=\"9-10\"><strong>steps 9–10</strong> <em>(two-hop question, bridge hidden)</em>: A two-hop question joins the two facts and hides the bridge: a, r<sub>1</sub>, r<sub>2</sub>, and the target is c. The bridge b never appears in the input, so the model has to recall it and use it internally. There is no chain of thought: the answer is the next token. This is purely implicit reasoning.</li><li data-steps=\"11-12\"><strong>steps 11–12</strong> <em>(knowledge graph)</em>: To get a real dataset, we build a knowledge graph with many entities and relations.</li><li data-steps=\"13-13\"><strong>step 13</strong> <em>(two graphs)</em>: To test generalization, we use two graphs with disjoint entities: the ID graph and the OOD graph.</li><li data-steps=\"14-14\"><strong>step 14</strong> <em>(shared relations)</em>: They share the same relations, but have completely different entities.</li><li data-steps=\"15-16\"><strong>steps 15–16</strong> <em>(train_atom)</em>: The training set has every atomic fact of both graphs.</li><li data-steps=\"17-17\"><strong>step 17</strong> <em>(train_id)</em>: Plus some two-hop questions, but only from the ID graph.</li><li data-steps=\"18-18\"><strong>step 18</strong> <em>(no OOD two-hop in training)</em>: No two-hop question from the OOD graph is ever trained.</li><li data-steps=\"19-19\"><strong>step 19</strong> <em>(test_id)</em>: The first test set, test_id: held-out two-hop questions on the ID graph.</li><li data-steps=\"20-20\"><strong>step 20</strong> <em>(test_ood)</em>: The second, test_ood: two-hop questions on the OOD graph. This one is much harder. Every OOD fact was trained only on its own, never as part of a composition. So the model has to apply the composition rule it learned on the ID graph to facts it only memorized in isolation.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> First, let's see how a standard Transformer does on this.</p><p><strong>Backup</strong></p><ul><li>Why the bridge is hidden: facts and questions are separate training examples. The model predicts c from (a, r<sub>1</sub>, r<sub>2</sub>) without generating b, so it must store the facts in its weights and compose them internally.</li><li>Sizes (§3.1): 500 entities per graph, 50 shared relations, every entity has exactly 10 outgoing edges with distinct relations: 5,000 atomic facts and 50,000 two-hop chains per graph. train_atom = all 10,000 facts; train_id = 10,000 questions from the ID graph; test_id = 2,000 held-out ID chains; test_ood = 2,000 questions from the OOD graph.</li><li>The two-graph protocol follows Wang et al. 2024.</li></ul><p><strong>Q&amp;A from the first talk</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Q</strong> (at step 14): Are the relations also different? Are the two graphs isomorphic?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Same relation vocabulary, disjoint entities. The two graphs are sampled independently, so they are not isomorphic and can have any shape. Only the statistics match: 500 entities, 10 outgoing edges each.</li></ul></li></ul>"
 },
 "6": {
  "title": "Vanilla Transformers fail to compose",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §3.1; §5.1 (non-looped baseline); Fig. 3 (left) at epoch 3,000</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> The setting: 500 entities per graph, 50 shared relations, 10 facts per entity, so 10,000 atomic facts in total, plus 10,000 two-hop training questions from the ID graph. The model is a standard 4-layer Transformer with d = 768, trained from scratch for 3,000 epochs, which is very long.</li><li data-steps=\"1-1\"><strong>step 1</strong> <em>(table)</em>: It gets 100% on both training sets: every atomic fact and every training two-hop question.</li><li data-steps=\"2-2\"><strong>step 2</strong> <em>(test_id)</em>: But only 16.9% on test_id…</li><li data-steps=\"3-3\"><strong>step 3</strong> <em>(test_ood)</em>: …and 0.8% on test_ood, essentially zero. It memorizes the training answers instead of learning to compose.</li><li data-steps=\"4-4\"><strong>step 4</strong> <em>(question)</em>: So why is it poor on both test sets, and so much worse on OOD?</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> To see why, let's look at where in the network each fact is stored.</p><p><strong>Backup</strong></p><ul><li>Tokenization: one token per entity and per relation; facts are 3 tokens, questions 4; the loss is on the last token.</li></ul>"
 },
 "7": {
  "title": "Knowledge storage in Transformers",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §1 (depth-local storage; Biran et al. 2024, Wang et al. 2024); Fig. 1 (middle left). Citations on screen at step 10: Ye et al. 2025; Wang et al. 2024</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> Think of a standard Transformer as a stack of layers, from shallow to deep. Each atomic fact is recalled at some depth.</li><li data-steps=\"1-1\"><strong>step 1</strong> <em>(ℓ<sub>1</sub> &lt; ℓ<sub>2</sub>)</em>: To answer in one pass, the first-hop fact, Alice's son is Bob, must be recalled in an earlier layer, and the second-hop fact, Bob's wife is Carol, must be available in a later layer.</li><li data-steps=\"2-3\"><strong>steps 2–3</strong> <em>(ℓ<sub>1</sub> = ℓ<sub>2</sub>, ℓ<sub>1</sub> &gt; ℓ<sub>2</sub>)</em>: But nothing forces this order. The two facts can sit at the same depth, or even in the reverse order. For OOD there is even less pressure, because those facts were only trained on their own, never in a composition.</li><li data-steps=\"4-4\"><strong>step 4</strong> <em>(token axis)</em>: Let's follow the reversed case along the tokens Alice, son, wife.</li><li data-steps=\"5-5\"><strong>step 5</strong> <em>(unused)</em>: At layer 2, the second-hop fact is there, but Bob hasn't been found yet. So it goes unused.</li><li data-steps=\"6-6\"><strong>step 6</strong> <em>(bridge)</em>: At layer 4, the first hop fires, and Bob appears at the \"son\" position.</li><li data-steps=\"7-8\"><strong>steps 7–8</strong> <em>(layers 5 and 6)</em>: Now layers 5 and 6 need Bob's wife, but they don't store that fact.</li><li data-steps=\"9-9\"><strong>step 9</strong> <em>(fact left behind)</em>: The fact was left behind at layer 2, and the forward pass can't go back up.</li><li data-steps=\"10-10\"><strong>step 10</strong> <em>(depth-local; citations)</em>: This is the depth-local storage problem: the second-hop fact is missing where it is needed. This explanation comes from prior work, and it's our starting point. Our question is how to fix it.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> If the fact is stuck at the wrong depth, a naive fix is to loop the same block, so every loop can read every fact.</p><p><strong>Backup</strong></p><ul><li>\"Hopping too late\" (Biran et al. 2024): the first hop is often resolved late, which leaves too few layers for the second hop.</li><li>ID is hard too: Wang et al. 2024 show that Transformers learn two-hop ID only through grokking (training far beyond overfitting), and fail completely on OOD.</li></ul><p><strong>Q&amp;A from the first talk</strong> (this slide got the most questions)</p><ul><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 4): How much should I believe this picture? Are facts really stored in different layers?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Take it as a cartoon of the depth-local storage hypothesis, not a measurement. Interpretability work suggests facts may be concentrated in a few layers, so \"fact 1 in layer 2, fact 2 in layer 6\" is not literally true. The robust finding is \"hopping too late\": the first hop often resolves late, leaving too little depth for the second.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 4): Did you inspect where the facts are stored in your toy model?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> No, we didn't; we rely on prior work for that. The precise claim: if a fact never appears as a second hop in training, which is the OOD case, nothing pushes it to be retrievable in the late layers where the second hop happens. So OOD composition fails.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 4): Which facts end up early and which late? Is it random?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> In our data all facts are symmetric (random graphs), so no fact is special. Where a fact ends up is not determined by its content: effectively arbitrary.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (end of slide): Do you use chain of thought?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> No. The model outputs the answer directly as the next token; that is the point of implicit reasoning. You can think of looping, which comes next, as a hidden CoT in depth.</li></ul></li></ul>"
 },
 "8": {
  "title": "Looped Transformer",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §3.1, Eq. (2), K = 2</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> Here is the Transformer with the bad storage order: the first-hop fact at layer 4, the second-hop fact at layer 2.</li><li data-steps=\"1-1\"><strong>step 1</strong> <em>(fold)</em>: Now fold the whole stack into one block, f<sub>θ</sub>. Both facts are in its memory.</li><li data-steps=\"2-2\"><strong>step 2</strong> <em>(two loops)</em>: And apply this same block twice: loop 1 and loop 2, with the same weights.</li><li data-steps=\"3-3\"><strong>step 3</strong> <em>(equation)</em>: The update is simple. H<sup>(0)</sup> is the token embeddings, and H<sup>(k+1)</sup> = f<sub>θ</sub>(H<sup>(k)</sup>). We use K = 2 loops, the maximum reasoning depth in the data.</li><li data-steps=\"4-4\"><strong>step 4</strong> <em>(token axis)</em>: Let's run the question Alice, son, wife.</li><li data-steps=\"5-6\"><strong>steps 5–6</strong> <em>(loop 1)</em>: In loop 1, the first-hop fact fires, and Bob appears at the \"son\" position.</li><li data-steps=\"7-8\"><strong>steps 7–8</strong> <em>(loop 2)</em>: In loop 2, the same block runs again, and now the second-hop fact is usable: Bob plus wife gives Carol.</li><li data-steps=\"9-9\"><strong>step 9</strong> <em>(same memory)</em>: Both loops read the same memory, so the storage order doesn't matter anymore. In principle, nothing is missing.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> Does it work in practice? Let's put it next to the non-looped model.</p><p><strong>Q&amp;A from the first talk</strong> (asked after the talk; they fit here)</p><ul><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 6): What is the relation between looped Transformers and latent CoT? Latent CoT doesn't decode either.<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> The main difference is supervision. Latent-CoT methods like Coconut are trained from CoT data: a curriculum replaces CoT steps with latent thoughts and supervises the remaining steps. A looped model only gets the final answer; its intermediate hidden states get no supervision. (Also: latent CoT adds positions, step after step; looping adds depth at the same positions.)</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 9): But Coconut doesn't supervise the latent thoughts either, right?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Right, there is no loss on the latent thoughts themselves: Coconut masks CoT steps and turns them into thinking tokens. But training still needs CoT data, and the language steps after the latent ones are supervised. Looping only needs question–answer pairs.</li></ul></li></ul>"
 },
 "9": {
  "title": "Looped vs. non-looped Transformer",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §3.2; §5.1 (baselines); Fig. 3 (left, baselines only); Table 1a (stage 2)</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> Same data, two models, with the same compute. The non-looped baseline has 4 layers: two untied copies of a 2-layer block. The looped model is that 2-layer block, applied twice. Same FLOPs, and the looped model has half the parameters.</li><li data-steps=\"1-1\"><strong>step 1</strong> <em>(curves)</em>: The looped model is much better on ID. It keeps climbing over thousands of epochs, but slowly. The non-looped model stays below 20%.</li><li data-steps=\"2-3\"><strong>steps 2–3</strong> <em>(final accuracy; OOD still low)</em>: At epoch 3,000: the vanilla loop gets 71.1% ID and 8.3% OOD; no loop gets 16.9% and 0.8%. Looping helps a lot, with half the parameters, but OOD is still only 8.3%.</li><li data-steps=\"4-4\"><strong>step 4</strong> <em>(question)</em>: So why does the vanilla loop still fail?</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> If looping gives both hops the same memory, why does it still fail? Let's look at what loop 1 actually hands to loop 2.</p><p><strong>Q&amp;A from the first talk</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 4): Do you keep the model size the same? What do you control?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Compute. No loop = 4 layers; looped = a 2-layer block × 2 loops, so the same FLOPs. The non-looped model has twice the parameters (§5.1: it upper-bounds the looped models' parameter count). <em>Careful: last time I first said it the wrong way round.</em></li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 2): Then shouldn't the looped model do worse?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> It has half the parameters and still does much better. Looping is a better inductive bias for composition, because the second hop can use the same memory as the first.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 2): Does looping hurt on other, non-reasoning tasks? Is that something you give up?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Pretraining results come on slide 12: DiscoLoop beats the vanilla loop on 6 of 7 standard benchmarks and ties on the 7th. More generally, looping adds compute, not parameters, so it does not add knowledge capacity; the Ouro paper (Zhu et al. 2025) reports that looped LMs gain from better knowledge manipulation, not capacity. We did not compare with non-looped LMs in pretraining.</li></ul></li></ul>"
 },
 "10": {
  "title": "Mechanism: stage-1 prediction → bridge → hidden state vs. embedding → intervention",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §3.2; Table 1; Eq. (3); Fig. 2. The deck numbers positions from 1 (bridge at H<sup>(1)</sup><sub>2</sub>); the paper numbers them from 0 (H<sup>(1)</sup><sub>1</sub>).</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-1\"><strong>steps 0–1</strong> <em>(Normal prediction)</em>: Let's do a mechanistic analysis of the looped Transformer. Here it is with K = 2, on the question Alice, son, wife. Loop 1 gives hidden states H<sup>(1)</sup>, loop 2 gives H<sup>(2)</sup>.</li><li data-steps=\"2-3\"><strong>steps 2–3</strong> <em>(read Carol)</em>: The normal prediction reads the last position after loop 2: multiply by the embedding matrix W to get logits, take the argmax: Carol, which matches the target.</li><li data-steps=\"4-4\"><strong>step 4</strong> <em>(Stage-1 prediction)</em>: Now our diagnostic, the stage-1 prediction: we stop after loop 1, so f<sub>θ</sub> runs only once, and read out the same position.</li><li data-steps=\"5-6\"><strong>steps 5–6</strong> <em>(Eve ≠ Carol)</em>: On the two-hop question, one loop gives Eve, not Carol. One loop is not enough.</li><li data-steps=\"7-9\"><strong>steps 7–9</strong> <em>(atomic fact: Bob)</em>: But on an atomic fact, Alice, son, question mark, one loop already gives Bob. Correct.</li><li data-steps=\"10-11\"><strong>steps 10–11</strong> <em>(stage-1 bars)</em>: Over the four splits, stage 1 gets 100% on atomic facts, but only 8.8% on the training two-hop questions, 0.9% on test_id and 0% on test_ood.</li><li data-steps=\"12-12\"><strong>step 12</strong> <em>(stage-2 bars)</em>: Stage 2, the normal prediction: 100, 100, 71.1 and 8.3.</li><li data-steps=\"13-13\"><strong>step 13</strong> <em>(one hop)</em>: So atomic facts come out of a single loop: the memory is fine. And the model does not shortcut two-hop questions inside one loop; loop 2 does the composition, as designed. The structure is right, but the generalization is not.</li><li data-steps=\"14-15\"><strong>steps 14–15</strong> <em>(Bridge after loop 1)</em>: Next question: after loop 1, does the model already know the bridge?</li><li data-steps=\"16-16\"><strong>step 16</strong> <em>(bridge position)</em>: Look at the \"son\" position, the r<sub>1</sub> token, where Bob should appear.</li><li data-steps=\"17-18\"><strong>steps 17–18</strong> <em>(logits, softmax)</em>: Apply the LM head and a softmax: almost all the probability is on Bob.</li><li data-steps=\"19-19\"><strong>step 19</strong> <em>(Table 1b)</em>: On the test sets, the probability of the true bridge is 1.000, on both ID and OOD.</li><li data-steps=\"20-20\"><strong>step 20</strong> <em>(bridge decodable)</em>: So the bridge is perfectly decodable after loop 1, and one loop recalls any atomic fact. Loop 2 has everything it needs; the storage problem is solved. So what is still wrong?</li><li data-steps=\"21-22\"><strong>steps 21–22</strong> <em>(Hidden state vs. embedding)</em>: The catch: loop 2 does not receive the token Bob. It receives this hidden state, a noisy continuous vector. Is it the same as the clean embedding of Bob?</li><li data-steps=\"23-23\"><strong>step 23</strong> <em>(readout chain)</em>: The readout only sees the hidden state through the logits and a softmax.</li><li data-steps=\"24-25\"><strong>steps 24–25</strong> <em>(+c; softmax invariance)</em>: And softmax ignores any shift that is the same for every token: softmax(z + c) = softmax(z). More generally, \"probability one on Bob\" only says that Bob's logit beats the others. It says little about the rest of the vector.</li><li data-steps=\"26-28\"><strong>steps 26–28</strong> <em>(two spaces, same readout)</em>: So the hidden state and the embedding can live in very different places and still decode to the same Bob.</li><li data-steps=\"29-30\"><strong>steps 29–30</strong> <em>(cosine)</em>: We measured it: the cosine similarity between this hidden state and the embedding of Bob is only 0.327 on test_id and 0.266 on test_ood.</li><li data-steps=\"31-31\"><strong>step 31</strong> <em>(≠)</em>: So the hidden state is not the embedding. A normal Transformer only ever reads clean embeddings. A looped one reads clean embeddings in loop 1, but noisy hidden states in loop 2: two different kinds of input for the same f<sub>θ</sub>.</li><li data-steps=\"32-32\"><strong>step 32</strong> <em>(Training-free intervention)</em>: If this mismatch is the problem, making the hidden state more like an embedding should help. And we can test that without any training.</li><li data-steps=\"33-33\"><strong>step 33</strong> <em>(Eq. 3)</em>: Between the two loops, at the bridge position only, we replace the hidden state by a mix: one minus alpha times itself, plus alpha times the normalized embedding of the token it decodes to.</li><li data-steps=\"34-35\"><strong>steps 34–35</strong> <em>(mix, then loop 2)</em>: So we mix in the clean embedding of the token the model itself decoded, and then run loop 2 as usual.</li><li data-steps=\"36-37\"><strong>steps 36–37</strong> <em>(α sweep)</em>: Now sweep alpha from 0 to 1. At 0 it's the vanilla model: about 70% ID and 8% OOD. Alpha 0.2 already triples OOD, to 26%. At 0.6 both are above 95%, and around 0.7 to 0.8 both are about 99%. More than ten times the OOD accuracy, with the same trained weights.</li><li data-steps=\"38-38\"><strong>step 38</strong> <em>(misaligned representation)</em>: So the bottleneck is a misaligned representation, not missing knowledge. What loop 1 hands to loop 2 is decodable, but not reusable.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> This intervention is placed by hand: one position, a hard argmax, an alpha we picked. DiscoLoop turns the same idea into a learned, differentiable channel at every position and every loop.</p><p><strong>Backup</strong></p><ul><li>Why stage 1 is a diagnostic: it separates \"the model found a one-loop shortcut\" from \"loop 2 composes the facts\". train_id: 8.8% at stage 1 vs. 100% at stage 2.</li><li>Why OOD is less aligned (0.266 vs. 0.327): OOD entities never act as bridges in training, so nothing pushes f<sub>θ</sub> to make their post-loop-1 states look like embeddings.</li><li>Fig. 2 values, α = 0 → 0.2 → 0.5 → 0.6 → 0.8: OOD 8 → 26 → 87 → 96 → 99%; ID 70 → 84 → 97 → 99 → 99.8%.</li><li>Don't quote the paper's text \"α = 0.1 → 25.9%\": on Fig. 2, and on this chart, 25.9% is at α = 0.2 (α = 0.1 gives about 15%).</li></ul><p><strong>Q&amp;A from the first talk</strong></p><ul><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 4): After loop 1, is it a noisy Bob or a clean Bob?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Noisy. It is the hidden state; the vanilla loop never turns it into a token. Loop 2 consumes it directly: clean embeddings in loop 1, hidden states in loop 2.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (at step 30): What is the dimension? Is a cosine of 0.3 high or low?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> d = 768. For reference, two random directions in 768 dimensions have a cosine of about 0 ± 0.04, so 0.3 clearly points toward Bob. But cos² ≈ 0.1: about 90% of the vector is something other than the Bob embedding.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 4): So the diagnosis is that OOD is much less aligned than ID?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> That is not the main claim; both are far from 1. OOD is lower (0.266 vs. 0.327), which fits OOD entities never being bridges in training. But the causal evidence is the intervention, not the cosine gap.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 4): The model sees noisy Bobs during training. Why can't it learn to handle them? Is it expressivity, optimization or generalization?<ul><li><strong>A (me):</strong> In principle, training can teach f<sub>θ</sub> to read both kinds of input, and it does on the training set. But it takes very long, like grokking, and it still doesn't transfer to OOD.</li><li><strong>A (Speaker 5):</strong> The model learns to read the noisy states it was trained on. OOD entities never occur as the bridge in a training question, so loop 2 has never seen their noisy hidden states.</li><li><strong>Bottom line:</strong> Not expressivity: train accuracy is 100% although the training hidden states are just as noisy, and the intervention shows that the same weights answer OOD questions once the bridge is cleaner. Not optimization of the training loss: it goes to about zero while test accuracy keeps changing. It is generalization (ID and especially OOD), plus slow optimization toward the generalizing solution, which DiscoLoop speeds up.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 2, at step 33): Do you mix in the embedding of the ground-truth Bob, or of the decoded token?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> The decoded one: b<sub>max</sub> = argmax of the readout of the hidden state. No label is used. Since the probability of Bob is 1.000, the decoded token is almost always the true bridge, so in practice they are the same.</li></ul></li></ul>"
 },
 "11": {
  "title": "DiscoLoop",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §4, Eqs. (4)–(6); Fig. 1 (right). Gate and τ: §5.1, §5.2, App. A.2, App. B</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0</strong> <em>(both channels)</em>: So here is the design principle: between loops, the residual stream should carry both the discrete embedding and the continuous hidden state.</li><li data-steps=\"1-1\"><strong>step 1</strong> <em>(name)</em>: That gives the name: Dis, co, Loop. Discrete, continuous, loop.</li><li data-steps=\"2-3\"><strong>steps 2–3</strong> <em>(loop 1, bridge state h)</em>: Back to the looped model. After loop 1, take a hidden state h, here at the bridge position.</li><li data-steps=\"4-5\"><strong>steps 4–5</strong> <em>(decode)</em>: First we decode it: logits W h, then a softmax with a temperature τ. This gives a distribution over tokens, mostly on Bob.</li><li data-steps=\"6-7\"><strong>steps 6–7</strong> <em>(encode)</em>: Then we encode it back: instead of taking the argmax, we take the probability-weighted sum of all token embeddings. That is a soft, differentiable version of \"replace h by the embedding of Bob\".</li><li data-steps=\"8-8\"><strong>step 8</strong> <em>(RMSNorm, gate)</em>: We normalize it with RMSNorm and scale it by a gate, alpha…</li><li data-steps=\"9-10\"><strong>steps 9–10</strong> <em>(add back)</em>: …and add it to the hidden state. Added, not swapped: h keeps its continuous information and gains a clean embedding part. This new h̃ is what loop 2 reads.</li><li data-steps=\"11-12\"><strong>steps 11–12</strong> <em>(Φ)</em>: Decode-then-encode is one operator, Φ. The whole step is: h, Φ, RMSNorm, gate, add.</li><li data-steps=\"13-13\"><strong>step 13</strong> <em>(Eq. 5)</em>: Formally, Φ(h) is the sum over tokens of p<sub>v</sub>(h) times W[v], where p(h) is the softmax of W h over τ.</li><li data-steps=\"14-15\"><strong>steps 14–15</strong> <em>(every position)</em>: We do this at every position, not only at the bridge. The model doesn't know where the bridge is.</li><li data-steps=\"16-18\"><strong>steps 16–18</strong> <em>(Eq. 4)</em>: The full recurrence: start from the token embeddings. Each loop computes H<sup>(k+1)</sup> = f<sub>θ</sub>(H̃<sup>(k)</sup>), and then adds the gated, normalized Φ of it to get H̃<sup>(k+1)</sup>.</li><li data-steps=\"19-19\"><strong>step 19</strong> <em>(gate)</em>: The gate alpha is token-wise: it sets how strongly we inject at each position.</li><li data-steps=\"20-20\"><strong>step 20</strong> <em>(fixed gate)</em>: The simplest choice is a fixed gate, the same value everywhere. On the symbolic tasks we use alpha = 1.</li><li data-steps=\"21-22\"><strong>steps 21–22</strong> <em>(learnable gate)</em>: Or a learnable gate, computed from the decoded content: a sigmoid of a linear function of Φ(h). It can open where the decoded distribution is informative, like at the bridge, and close elsewhere. It adds only d + 1 parameters.</li><li data-steps=\"23-23\"><strong>step 23</strong> <em>(last loop)</em>: After the last loop the gate is zero, so the prediction is read directly from the last hidden states, like in a normal model.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> Does DiscoLoop work? We test it in three settings: the symbolic task we already know, a natural-language version, and real pretraining.</p><p><strong>Backup</strong></p><ul><li>Compared with the intervention: trained, not training-free; soft and differentiable, not an argmax; every position and every loop, not one position; added, not a convex mix. As τ → 0, Φ(h) becomes the hard W[v<sub>max</sub>].</li><li>Cost: Φ needs one extra LM-head pass per loop. In pretraining we approximate Φ with the top-k tokens, k = 128.</li><li>Settings: symbolic two-hop and three-hop use the fixed gate α = 1, τ = 1; the natural-language tasks use the learnable gate.</li></ul><p><strong>Q&amp;A from the first talk</strong> (asked after the talk)</p><ul><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 8): What is the difference between the constant alpha and the learned gate?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> The fixed gate, alpha = 1, is what we use on the symbolic tasks: simple and stable. The learned gate is used on the natural-language tasks, and it works much better in real pretraining: real text has many uncertain positions, where the soft embedding is a blurry average, and the gate learns to inject only where the decoded distribution is informative.</li></ul></li></ul>"
 },
 "12": {
  "title": "Experiments: symbolic · natural language · pretraining",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §5.1 (Fig. 3); §5.2 (Fig. 4); §5.3 (Table 2, Fig. 7); App. A.2; App. B</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-1\"><strong>steps 0–1</strong> <em>(symbolic: test accuracy)</em>: First, the same symbolic task. DiscoLoop is orange, the vanilla loop blue, no loop grey; solid lines are ID, dashed lines OOD. DiscoLoop reaches near-perfect accuracy on both, about 99% ID and 99% OOD, against 71% and 8% for the vanilla loop. And it is much faster: there is a sharp jump around epoch 500, and after it both ID and OOD are above 80%.</li><li data-steps=\"2-4\"><strong>steps 2–4</strong> <em>(recap: stage-1 train accuracy)</em>: To see what happens during training, we track the stage-1 training accuracy: take a training two-hop question, run one loop, decode, and compare with the target. If it is high, the answer is memorized inside a single loop.</li><li data-steps=\"5-5\"><strong>step 5</strong> <em>(stage-1 curves)</em>: Early on, every model answers its training two-hop questions in one loop: it memorizes them. No loop stays at 100%, because it has no second loop to hand the work to. The looped models push stage-1 accuracy down, and DiscoLoop does it much earlier and much more sharply.</li><li data-steps=\"6-7\"><strong>steps 6–7</strong> <em>(memorize → decompose)</em>: For DiscoLoop, stage 1 is high at first, which is memorization. Then it collapses, exactly when the test accuracy jumps: the model starts to decompose the question into hops.</li><li data-steps=\"8-8\"><strong>step 8</strong> <em>(caption)</em>: So looping gives an inductive bias from memorizing two-hop answers to decomposing them into hops, and DiscoLoop's cleaner signal makes it stronger: an earlier transition and a higher final accuracy.</li><li data-steps=\"9-9\"><strong>step 9</strong> <em>(natural language: vocabulary)</em>: To check that this is not an artifact of symbolic tokens, we write the same task in English: entity tokens become names, and relation tokens become words like teacher and wife.</li><li data-steps=\"10-10\"><strong>step 10</strong> <em>(atomic fact)</em>: An atomic fact: \"Finley's teacher is Anya.\"</li><li data-steps=\"11-11\"><strong>step 11</strong> <em>(direct)</em>: A two-hop question in the direct format: \"Finley's teacher's wife is Charlie.\"</li><li data-steps=\"12-13\"><strong>steps 12–13</strong> <em>(reverse)</em>: And in a reverse, QA-style format: \"Who is the wife of the teacher of Finley? Answer: Charlie.\" Same facts and same answer, but the relations come in the opposite order from the order of composition.</li><li data-steps=\"14-14\"><strong>step 14</strong> <em>(Fig. 4)</em>: Same picture in both formats. DiscoLoop reaches near 100% ID and about 95% OOD. The vanilla loop gets about 90% ID but fails on OOD, especially in the reverse format. No loop gets about 40% ID on direct and 20% on reverse, and essentially zero OOD. DiscoLoop is also much faster: perfect ID by around epoch 500.</li><li data-steps=\"15-15\"><strong>step 15</strong> <em>(pretraining setup)</em>: Finally, real pretraining. We use the Ouro looped architecture: 24 layers, d = 1024, looped 4 times, 440M parameters, trained on 20B tokens of FineWeb-Edu and FineMath, mixed 6 to 4. FineMath makes the data more reasoning-heavy. We compare the vanilla loop with DiscoLoop; everything else is identical.</li><li data-steps=\"16-17\"><strong>steps 16–17</strong> <em>(loss; zoom)</em>: Here is the training loss over 20B tokens. Let's zoom in on the second half.</li><li data-steps=\"18-18\"><strong>step 18</strong> <em>(crossover)</em>: The vanilla loop is slightly ahead early on, but DiscoLoop crosses below it at about 13B tokens and stays lower until the end.</li><li data-steps=\"19-19\"><strong>step 19</strong> <em>(Table 2)</em>: On zero-shot benchmarks, DiscoLoop is better on six of seven and tied on HellaSwag. The average goes from 49.3 to 50.5, with the biggest gains on ARC-C, LAMBADA and SciQ, about two points each. Same backbone, data and number of loops; the only extra cost is the decode-then-encode, which we approximate with the top 128 tokens.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> Let me put it all together.</p><p><strong>Backup</strong></p><ul><li>Symbolic at epoch 3,000 (from the logs behind the chart): DiscoLoop 99.8% ID / 98.8% OOD; vanilla loop 71.1 / 8.3; no loop 16.9 / 0.8.</li><li>Three-hop (App. A.2; K = 3, trained on two- and three-hop): DiscoLoop is near 100% ID on both, about 90% OOD on two-hop and 65% OOD on three-hop. The vanilla loop gets about 40% ID / 5% OOD on two-hop, and both baselines stay near 0 on three-hop OOD. The gap grows with depth: the mismatch compounds at every hand-off.</li><li>PonderLM (in the paper, not on the slide) keeps the recurrence only in embedding space: average 49.8, vs. 50.5 for DiscoLoop and 49.3 for the vanilla loop. PonderLM is best on LAMBADA (38.0 vs. 37.6).</li><li>Gains vs. the vanilla loop: ARC-C +2.1, LAMBADA +2.5, SciQ +2.0, PIQA +1.0, RACE +0.3, ARC-E +0.4, HellaSwag 0.0. The largest loss gap is about 0.01.</li><li>Pretraining details (App. B): 16 heads, context 8192, DeepSeek-V3 tokenizer, AdamW + Muon, 19,074 steps; every recurrent model applies the backbone 4 times.</li><li>Natural-language runs: 5,000 epochs, learnable gate.</li></ul>"
 },
 "13": {
  "title": "Conclusion",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §6</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> To conclude.</li><li data-steps=\"1-1\"><strong>step 1</strong> <em>(takeaway 1)</em>: First, a mechanistic analysis of implicit multi-hop reasoning. In a standard Transformer, facts stored in earlier layers are not available where the second hop happens. Looping fixes this storage problem, because the same block, with the same memory, runs in every loop. But it leaves a representation bottleneck: what loop 1 hands to loop 2 is decodable, but not aligned with the embedding space.</li><li data-steps=\"2-2\"><strong>step 2</strong> <em>(takeaway 2)</em>: Second, DiscoLoop: loop both the discrete embedding and the continuous hidden state. It nearly solves two-hop reasoning on ID and OOD, trains much faster, and improves real pretraining.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> Two open questions to finish.</p>"
 },
 "14": {
  "title": "Discussions: length generalization · scalable looping",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: §6 (length generalization as future work); App. A.2. \"Scalable looping\" is our outlook, not in the paper; SPS = Monea et al. 2026, arXiv 2607.01218, Fig. 1</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-3\"><strong>steps 0–3</strong> <em>(train: one-hop and two-hop, K = 2)</em>: First, length generalization. Say we train on one-hop and two-hop questions with two loops.</li><li data-steps=\"4-6\"><strong>steps 4–6</strong> <em>(test: three-hop, one more loop)</em>: At test time we ask a three-hop question. Can we just add one more loop, K = 3, with the same f<sub>θ</sub>, and get the answer?</li><li data-steps=\"7-7\"><strong>step 7</strong> <em>(test-time scaling)</em>: If yes, harder questions simply get more loops, which is a kind of test-time scaling. We don't know yet. It would be a stronger test of whether the model learns a general composition procedure, not a fixed-depth one.</li><li data-steps=\"8-10\"><strong>steps 8–10</strong> <em>(scalable looping: more loops)</em>: Second, scalable looping. Loops are a new axis for scaling compute with the same parameters: one, two, four, eight loops.</li><li data-steps=\"11-12\"><strong>steps 11–12</strong> <em>(deep graph)</em>: But many loops make a very deep network. With 24 layers and 8 loops, you have almost 200 layers, and the gradients fade or blow up. It is hard to optimize.</li><li data-steps=\"13-16\"><strong>steps 13–16</strong> <em>(SPS)</em>: Another direction is extra tokens instead of extra depth. A recent example is State-Prediction Separation: after every input token, they insert a \"predict\" token, and the next token is predicted there. The predict tokens' KV entries are dropped after a short window. So each predicted token gets two positions of compute.</li><li data-steps=\"17-17\"><strong>step 17</strong> <em>(question)</em>: Is there a more scalable way to loop? That is what we are thinking about next.</li></ul><p><strong>Transition:</strong> That's all from me. Thank you!</p><p><strong>Backup</strong></p><ul><li>The three-hop experiment (App. A.2) trains on three-hop questions with K = 3, so it is not extrapolation.</li></ul><p><strong>Q&amp;A from the first talk</strong> (asked after the talk; they fit here)</p><ul><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 2): Short latent reasoning is much cheaper than a looped model, which has to run every layer each time; you can't skip a layer.<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Yes, there are trade-offs. Other looping designs only loop a few middle layers, and there are efficiency considerations in choosing what to loop.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 8): CoT is adaptive: easy problems get a short CoT. Can looping be adaptive too?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Yes. Ouro learns an exit gate: when the model is already confident in its answer, it stops looping and outputs (early exit), so easy inputs use fewer loops.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speakers 4 and 9): Looped models share weights across all layers. Why loop the whole Transformer and not a few selected layers? Is there a better way to choose which layers to loop?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> That makes a lot of sense. One idea is attention over depth, like attention-style residual connections across layers and loops, so a later loop can go back and fetch information it missed in earlier layers.</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 6): If a company wants to train a looped model, does it need new SFT data, or just more training?<ul><li><strong>A:</strong> Frontier labs would more likely add looping in mid-training than pretrain a looped model from scratch, and they use a lot of reasoning SFT data. Whether they reshape the data for looping, I'm not sure, but it would make sense. (Our paper lists continual pretraining of existing models into looped ones as future work.)</li></ul></li><li><strong>Q</strong> (Speaker 2): Then how does the final model's CoT become very short, or empty?<ul><li><strong>A (Speaker 5):</strong> RL with a length penalty, among other ways.</li><li><strong>A (me):</strong> Looping might also shorten the reasoning naturally, but I'm not sure. SPS uses two positions for each predicted token: more compute per token, which could allow fewer tokens.</li></ul></li></ul>"
 },
 "15": {
  "title": "Thank you!",
  "html": "<p class=\"paper\"><em>Paper: none. Below: one line per question asked after the first talk (details on the topic slides and in FAQ.md)</em></p><ul><li data-steps=\"0-0\"><strong>step 0:</strong> Thank you! I'm happy to take questions.</li></ul><p><strong>Q&amp;A cheat sheet</strong></p><ul><li><strong>How did you come up with the idea?</strong> (Speaker 7) Collaboration: Hanlin has worked on LLM reasoning for a long time, and others on the team work on looped Transformers. The idea came from experiments: synthetic runs showed the mismatch, then we asked how to fix the looping architecture.</li><li><strong>Method first, interpretation later?</strong> (Speaker 6) Iterative. We tried many fixes for the two-hop task; interpretability experiments showed what the problem was, and the architecture came from that insight (intervention, then DiscoLoop).</li><li><strong>Constant vs. learned gate?</strong> (Speaker 8) Fixed alpha = 1 on the symbolic tasks (simple, stable); the learned gate on the natural-language tasks and in pretraining, where it is much better because it injects only where the decoding is informative.</li><li><strong>Looping vs. latent CoT?</strong> (Speaker 6) Supervision: latent CoT (Coconut) is trained from CoT data; looping only from the final answer. Latent CoT adds positions; looping adds depth.</li><li><strong>Coconut doesn't supervise latent thoughts?</strong> (Speaker 9) No direct loss on them, but the curriculum is built from CoT data and the later steps are supervised.</li><li><strong>Latent CoT is cheaper; loops can't skip layers.</strong> (Speaker 2) A real trade-off; some designs loop only a few middle layers.</li><li><strong>Can looping be adaptive, like CoT length?</strong> (Speaker 8) Yes: Ouro's learned exit gate stops looping once the model is confident.</li><li><strong>Loop only selected layers?</strong> (Speakers 4 and 9) Plausible; attention over depth could let later loops fetch information from earlier layers.</li><li><strong>Training a looped model in industry: new SFT data?</strong> (Speaker 6) Likely added in mid-training with reasoning SFT data; reshaping the data may help; continual pretraining is our future work.</li><li><strong>How does the CoT become short?</strong> (Speaker 2) RL with a length penalty (Speaker 5); looping or SPS spend more compute per token.</li><li><strong>Doesn't explain Astra?</strong> Right: Astra is motivation for architectures that reason without CoT.</li><li><strong>Why not just a long CoT?</strong> Inefficient: verbose, costly for long-horizon tasks and RL.</li><li><strong>Why not split the problem into sub-steps for separate models or agents?</strong> (Speaker 9) Can work for specific tasks, but not sure in general. Loops can be cheap: for graph reachability (start node to end node), loops can compose hops in parallel, so the number of loops can grow like log of the number of steps. (Speaker 4 added: the lesson of LM pretraining is that a general left-to-right model is a strong enough inductive bias; focus on optimization, not task-specific pipelines.)</li><li><strong>Did you use AI to make the presentation?</strong> (Speaker 2) Yes.</li><li><strong>Suggestion</strong> (Speaker 2): reproduce an Astra-style no-CoT test with a looped LM.</li></ul>"
 }
};
